---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>The argument</h2>
  <p>
    The annoying thing about a spiral isn't that it happens. It's that it{" "}
    <em>repeats</em> — the same thought back at 2am, having made no progress
    since 11pm. This course argues that a loop keeps re-running because it
    never gets the conditions under which thinking actually finishes, and that
    those conditions are oddly specific. Which would mean the problem was
    never thinking too much. It's that the thinking never gets to complete —
    which is where the course gets its name, and the second reading of that
    name is deliberate too.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    A crit and a lecture every week, for twelve weeks. Each crit hands you a
    small provocation and turns you loose on it with the rest of the room —
    overanalysing a text message in layers, guessing the ending of something a
    classmate overthought, making and eating a sandwich in complete silence,
    running the same excessive rigour on a best-case scenario instead of a
    worst one. The lectures, held outdoors, supply the research the argument
    rests on. Every class opens with ten minutes of nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What it isn't</h2>
  <p>
    Not a wellness course, and not therapy. The science here is real and
    cited, including the parts where the evidence is thinner than the internet
    suggests — you will be told which claims are findings and which are
    someone's hunch. The course's own central claim is labelled as a hunch,
    because that is what it is. It also takes almost nothing seriously, which
    turns out to be the point rather than a tone choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who has re-read a two-word reply more times than it deserved, or
    shelved a decision because thinking about it further felt like progress.
    No background in psychology or philosophy assumed — the only prerequisite
    is a mind willing to catch itself in the act. Nothing you share is marked,
    and you can pass on any activity without giving a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What happens in each crit, and what to bring.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The outdoor lectures, and the research behind them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
